{"name":"Violet Extract","hex":"#dee2ec","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"violet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant or flower of the genus Viola, especially the fragrant Viola odorata;  similar-looking plants and flowers.","A person thought to resemble V. odorata, especially in its beauty and delicacy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a violet colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/violet"},{"term":"extract","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something that is extracted or drawn out.","A portion of a book, document, recording etc. incorporated distinctly in another work (for written or spoken words, synonymous to a citation; a quotation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To draw out; to pull out; to remove forcibly from a fixed position, as by traction or suction, etc.","To withdraw by squeezing, distillation, or other mechanical or chemical process. Compare abstract (transitive verb)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/extract"}],"entries":[{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violet_(color)"},{"title":"Extract","blocks":[{"text":"An extract (essence) is a substance made by extracting a part of a raw material, often by using a solvent such as ethanol, oil or water. Extracts may be sold as tinctures or absolutes or dried and powdered."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Vanilla_extract.JP